Path planning in complex three-dimensional (3D) environments remains a fundamental computational bottleneck for autonomous mobile robots, Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs), and spatial simulation engines. While classic graph-search algorithms like "A" ^* provide strong guarantees of optimality and completeness, their performance degrades exponentially in high-resolution 3D spaces due to the combinatorial explosion of explored nodes. Standard geometric heuristics (e.g., Euclidean distance) fail to account for environment-specific topological barriers, forcing uninformed exploration into unpromising regions. In this paper, we propose NACHA* (Neural-Adaptive Cost-Heuristic "A" ^*), a hybrid search paradigm that unifies data-driven neural potential fields with classic graph search. By introducing a fine-tuned Adaptive Cost-Shaping mechanism regulated by a parameter α, NACHA* injects environmental context directly into the search heuristic without sacrificing safety or completeness. We evaluate NACHA* across extensive standardized 3D benchmark environments, ranging from grid resolutions of 20×20×20 (8,000 states) up to scale resolutions of 50×50×50 (125,000 states). Experimental results demonstrate that NACHA* achieves a speedup in search runtime of up to 80.72× in 20×20×20 grid resolutions and up to 1,252.97× in 50×50×50 scale maps, reduces node expansions by up to 99.7% compared to baseline "A" ^* while maintaining absolute path optimality. Furthermore, we provide a rigorous analysis of failure modes in low-density maps and dense maze topologies, offering automated fallback solutions.
